eg. Jupyter Notebook
pypdf — Extracted raw text from a real government PDF document
Text Chunking — Split the document into overlapping 500-character segments
Sentence Transformers — Converted each chunk to a 384-dimensional vector using all-MiniLM-L6-v2
ChromaDB — Persisted all embeddings to disk in a queryable vector collection
PCA — Compressed 384-D vectors to 3-D while preserving as much variance as possible
Plotly — Rendered an interactive 3D scatter plot to explore semantic clusters

Key Takeaways:
Vectors encode meaning — similar chunks appear as neighboring points in vector space
Chunking with overlap prevents context loss at document boundaries
ChromaDB makes it trivial to store and retrieve high-dimensional vectors — no server required
PCA and t-SNE are complementary tools: PCA is fast and deterministic; t-SNE preserves local structure better for large datasets
